  "seven_q": {
    "Q0_posture": "Honest and self-critical; acknowledges fragility and potential collapse of the isomorphism",
    "Q1_identity": "An isomorphism record analyzing the structural parallel between observer participation in quantum mechanics and witness theology",
    "Q2_domain": "Physics (quantum measurement) and Christian theology (witness theology, participatio)",
    "Q3_claim": "Observation in quantum mechanics (measurement actualizing potential) is structurally isomorphic to witness theology (creation requiring observers for its meaning to be actualized)",
    "Q4_support": "Four shared structures: observation as constitutive, participation not independence, potential\u2192actual via participation, observers as structurally necessary; supported by experiments (delayed-choice) and theological tradition (Aquinas, Genesis)",
    "Q5_dependencies": "Depends on the measurement problem being unresolved (decoherence not fully explaining collapse), IIT being correct, and Wheeler's participatory universe being valid",
    "Q6_consequences": "If true, strengthens the case for human co-creation and the necessity of observers in the universe; if false, the mapping reverts to mere analogy",
    "Q7_kill": "Decoherence + Everett (many-worlds) fully resolving the measurement problem without any role for observers; IIT being proven wrong; Wheeler's participatory universe being falsified"
  },
